{# narrative_engine_plot_adapt.prompt — a scheme has hit a setback; this
   call decides whether the person behind it finds another way or gives up.
   Receives the JSON object produced by PlotAdapt::Compose:
     mastermind { name }, ambition,
     objective { type, verb, target, index }, history [string],
     failure { step, outcome, progress, caught }, attempt, max_attempts,
     actors [{ index, name, relation, standing }],
     items [{ index, name, category }], step_types [{ id, description }],
     min_steps, max_steps,
     npc { UUID } — required by SkyrimNet's character_profile submodules;
                    keys every character-bio decorator. #}
[ system ]
## Task
A scheme has hit a setback. You decide whether the person behind it finds
another way, or gives up.

Both answers are real answers. A schemer who never gives up is not persistent,
they are a machine — and a plot that grinds through every possible approach is
worse reading than one that ends because the person behind it decided it was
not worth it any more. Concede when a real person would.

**The objective does not change.** You are deciding how they get there, not
what they are after.

## What you may name

You do not invent people or objects. Everything you can name is given to you
with a number, and you refer to it by that number.

## The steps you may use

{% for s in step_types %}- `{{ s.id }}` — {{ s.description }}
{% endfor %}

## Output Format
Return a JSON object. No markdown fences. No prose before or after the object.

`decision` — either `"revise"` or `"concede"`.

**If `"concede"`**, add:

- `reason` — one sentence, third person, saying what made them stop. This is
  what a reader sees, so it should be about the person, not about the plan:
  "she decided the ledger was not worth what it would cost her" rather than
  "the acquire step failed".

**If `"revise"`**, add:

- `plan` — the remaining steps, in order: as many as this way to the objective
  actually needs and no more. Each has a `type` from the list above and a
  `target` number, taken from **Objects** when the type is `acquire` and from
  **People** otherwise.
- **The last entry must be the objective, exactly as it is given to you.** A
  plan that ends anywhere else is thrown away, and the scheme concedes by
  default.

A revision should react to what actually went wrong. If they were seen, deal
with the person who saw them. If the approach was too slow, take a shorter one.
Repeating the failed step unchanged is not a revision.

[ end system ]

[ user ]
## Whose scheme this is

{{ render_character_profile("full", npc.UUID) }}

**{{ mastermind.name }}** wants this:

> {{ ambition }}

The objective is **{{ objective.verb }} {{ objective.target }}** — type
`{{ objective.type }}`, target {{ objective.index }}. A revised plan must end
on exactly that.

## What has happened so far

{% if length(history) > 0 %}
{% for h in history %}- {{ h }}
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
Nothing yet; the first step is the one that went wrong.
{% endif %}

## The setback

**{{ failure.step }}** — {{ failure.outcome }}.

{% if length(failure.progress) > 0 %}They had got about {{ failure.progress }} of the way
through it when it went wrong.{% endif %}

{% if failure.caught %}
They were **seen**. Whatever comes next has to account for somebody now knowing
something they should not, and for that person having a reason to talk.
{% else %}
It simply did not work in time. Nobody knows it was attempted.
{% endif %}

This is attempt {{ attempt }} of at most {{ max_attempts }}. After that they
stop whether they want to or not, so a plan that needs three more setbacks to
pay off is not a plan.

## People
{% for a in actors %}{{ a.index }}. {{ a.name }} — {{ a.relation }}{% if length(a.standing) > 0 %}, {{ a.standing }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

{% if length(items) > 0 %}
## Objects
{% for i in items %}{{ i.index }}. {{ i.name }} ({{ i.category }})
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

Decide whether {{ mastermind.name }} finds another way to the same end, or
stops. Answer with the object.
[ end user ]
